{"entity":{"id":"hb:kingdom-of-norway@900","name":"Kingdom of Norway","dataset":"basemaps","kind":"kingdom"},"territories":[{"id":1682,"name":"Kingdom of Norway","set_id":"hb_900","class":"kingdom","valid_from":900,"valid_to":1000,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":222651.14697106072,"border_precision":1,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":null,"sovereign_note":null,"modern_name":"Norway","min_lon":4.980375322802313,"min_lat":57.97173651333513,"max_lon":20.358317408068427,"max_lat":69.78590735073747,"source_file":"world_900.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q2196956","from_t":900,"to_t":1000,"method":"name-label+date","confidence":0.95,"label":"Kingdom of Norway","description":"former Kingdom from 872 to 1397","inception":872,"dissolved":1397,"enwiki":"Kingdom of Norway (872–1397)","summary":"The term Norwegian Realm (Old Norse: *Noregsveldi, Bokmål: Norgesveldet, Nynorsk: Noregsveldi or Noregsveldet) and Old Kingdom of Norway refer to the Kingdom of Norway's peak of power in the 13th century after a long period of civil war before 1240. The kingdom was a loosely unified state including the territory of modern-day Norway, modern-day Swedish territory of Jämtland, Herjedalen, Ranrike (Bohuslän) and Idre and Särna, as well as Norway's overseas possessions which had been settled by Norwegian seafarers for centuries before being annexed or incorporated into the kingdom as 'tax territories'. To the North, Norway also bordered extensive tax territories on the mainland. Norway, whose expansionism starts from the very foundation of the Kingdom in 872, reached the peak of its power in the years between 1240 and 1319."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q2196956","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q3446236","value_label":"Kingdom of Vestmar","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P1365","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Kingdom of Vestmar"},{"qid":"Q2196956","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q16673773","value_label":"Kingdom of Agder","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"pr